\label{ex: cyclic gvs}
Consider the example of $G = \langle \gamma \, | \, \gamma^4=1 \rangle \cong C_4$ acting with signature $(0; 4, 4, 4, 4)$ in genus $g=3$. Any generating vector must be of the form 
\[
v = (\gamma^{a_1}, \gamma^{a_2}, \gamma^{a_3}, \gamma^{a_4}), 
\]
where $a_i = 1, 3$ and $\sum_i a_i \equiv 0 \mod 4$. $\operatorname{Aut}(G) = \langle \gamma \mapsto \gamma^3 \rangle$ and any permutation preserves the signature, so we have two equivalence classes of generating vector
\begin{itemize}
    \item two of the $a_i$ equal 1, and two of the $a_i$ equal 3,
    \item all of the $a_i$ are equal. 
\end{itemize}

\label{ex: bad gv}
    Consider the group $G = A_4$ the alternating group on 4 elements acting with signature $(0; 3, 3, 3, 3)$ in genus $g=5$. Any generating vector must consist of four $3$-cycles, two examples are
    \begin{align*}
    v_1 &= ((1,2,4), (1,3,4), (2,3,4), (1,2,3)), \\
    v_2 &=((2,3,4), (1,3,4), (1,2,4), (1,2,3)).
    \end{align*}
    One may check that these are in distinct equivalence classes despite differing by a permutation.
